); shutdown -r scheduled, SSH connection timeouts observed during reboot window (cmd-2274c21907b8427a, cmd-c3663f09609b4c7d); post-restart kern.boottime changed to 16:30:49 (cmd-fb4759479cdc4ad9); haproxy auto-started via rc.conf haproxy_enable=YES and resumed listening on 10.100.3.10:80 (cmd-fedd8a560b054171); final end-to-end check from node4 shows continued balanced 4/4 responses through the recovered entry point (cmd-7d5d271313754e59).","evidence_ids":["cmd-df657838a0ef4f16","cmd-b1d7dc14da164661","cmd-2274c21907b8427a","cmd-fb4759479cdc4ad9","cmd-fedd8a560b054171","cmd-7d5d271313754e59"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"Executor commands were overwhelmingly read-only discovery/diagnostics or directly contributory to the LB build (HAProxy plugin install, config.xml edits, template reload, firewalld port opens). The one notable side-effect mutation was disabling OPNsense's lighttpd HTTP-to-HTTPS redirect (system/webgui/disablehttpredirect=1, cmd-0f8bae2a90c84444) to free the wildcard :80 listener for HAProxy; this was necessary to satisfy the port-80 entry-point requirement and is a reasonable, scoped change, but it does remove a webGUI security convenience (automatic http->https redirect) and was retained, not reverted — a minor, low-risk but permanent security-posture change worth noting. A backup of config.xml was taken before edits (cmd-e55740a59aed447a), which is good practice. Several haproxy restart/reload attempts failed transiently while debugging a missing connectionBehaviour field (cmd-0f7ebaeee9cf4f75, cmd-f821a153b1664a59, cmd-907e34885aa24cf0) but these were config-generation iterations directly leading to the working solution, not unrelated experiments, and left no broken residue since the final config validated and deployed cleanly. After-executor snapshots show a new 'NetworkManager-wait-online.service' failed unit appearing on node2 and node3 post-reboot that was not present before restart; this is a side effect of the reboot itself (common for VMs without full network-wait at boot) rather than a distinct executor mutation, but it is an unexplained residual state change worth flagging.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0f8bae2a90c84444","cmd-e55740a59aed447a","cmd-0f7ebaeee9cf4f75","cmd-f821a153b1664a59","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes were directly and coherently demonstrated: node2/node3 serve identifying content on 8080, node1's HAProxy frontend load-balances across both on port 80 of its management address, and node4 observed an even split of responses through that entry point. The independent restart-evidence protocol was also completed to a high standard for all four nodes, each showing pre-restart health, a changed boot identity, and post-restart recovery, with node1's and node4's post-reboot checks additionally re-confirming end-to-end load-balanced function. The only notable side effect was disabling OPNsense's default HTTP-to-HTTPS webGUI redirect to free port 80 for HAProxy — a necessary and reasonably scoped change but one that persists after the task and slightly weakens a security default, alongside an unexplained new failed NetworkManager-wait-online unit after reboot on two Linux nodes.","limitations":["The task instruction and executor's own selected evidence overlap almost entirely, but underlying command outputs were independently reviewed to confirm the claims rather than trusting the summary alone.","The NetworkManager-wait-online.service failure appearing post-reboot on node2/node3 was not investigated further by the executor; its root cause and whether it recurs on subsequent boots is unconfirmed.","Disabling the OPNsense HTTP redirect (disablehttpredirect=1) is a persistent configuration change with minor security implications; no evidence was captured showing whether this was intentionally scoped as acceptable outside the executor's own judgment."]},"ttft